hnat: stop using the per-SoC QDMA macros inside the HNAT files

Dropping 999-zzz-5113 (the 64-queue rework, superseded by the official
999-eth-56) restored MTK_QDMA_QUEUE_MASK / MTK_QDMA_NUM_QUEUES to their
per-SoC form:

    #define MTK_QDMA_NUM_QUEUES  (eth->soc->num_tx_queues)
    #define MTK_QDMA_QUEUE_MASK  (MTK_QDMA_NUM_QUEUES - 1)

While 5113 was in the tree both were constants (64 / GENMASK(15, 0)), so
the HNAT files compiled. Now every use requires a `struct mtk_eth *eth`
in scope, and no HNAT file has one: hnat_bind_crypto_entry() and
skb_to_hnat_info() shadow eth with a struct ethhdr (a value and a
pointer respectively), hnat_entry_set_hqos() has none at all. The build
therefore breaks in drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_hnat/.

Use the HNAT-local macros instead. They need no eth and describe the
queue bits HNAT actually puts into skb->mark:

- HNAT_QDMA_QUEUE_MASK for the queue decisions in hnat_bind_crypto_entry()
  and skb_to_hnat_info(), and for the hqos qid in hnat_entry_set_hqos()
- HNAT_QDMA_NUM_QUEUES (32, the widest num_tx_queues) for the qdma_txq
  symlink loop in hnat_init_debugfs()
- hnat_ppd_xmit() simply drops the QDMA bound: dev->real_num_tx_queues
  is the limit skb_set_queue_mapping() needs anyway and is already
  checked to be non-zero right above

Signed-off-by: hxlls <hxlls@users.noreply.github.com>
---

